# ---- discussion
cut("H1('Discussion')", "H1('Conclusions')", r"""H1('Discussion')
H2('Statistical associations')
P(f'Three associations are robust within the present data. First, CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} selectivity in the dilute limit decreases with pore size, and frameworks with S > 20 are almost all ultramicroporous or carry strongly polar or charged groups. Second, the linkage class explains little of the variance once pore size is considered. Third, descriptor-based models that encode pore geometry predict selectivity more accurately than models without explicit pore information, including SchNet. These are correlations within one physical model (rigid frameworks, generic LJ parameters, EQeq charges and the Henry limit) and should not be extrapolated to other force fields, flexible frameworks or finite loading without further tests.')
H2('Mechanistic interpretation')
P(f'The DFT binding energies provide a mechanistic rationale for the first two associations: the five linkage motifs differ in intrinsic CO_{{2}} binding by only a few kJ mol^{{−1}}, whereas adsorption enthalpies in narrow pores are several times larger than any single-site binding energy, which points to multi-wall confinement as the main source of selectivity. This interpretation is limited by the small number of motifs, by the use of one adsorption site per motif, and by the generic force field used in the screening. The underestimated low-pressure uptake of the nitrogen-rich ACOF-1 and COF-JLU2 shows that the force field misses part of the binding at N-rich sites, so the contribution of chemistry is probably larger in reality than in our simulations.')
H2('Comparison with previous studies')
P(f'The dominant role of pore size agrees with the screening of 295 COFs by Altundal et al., who found the best CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} adsorbents among frameworks with pores below about 10 Å{cite("altundal2020")}, and with screens of hypothetical COFs{cite("deeg2020", "aksu2025")}; the present work extends these findings to the experimentally reported frameworks with quantified uncertainties and an explicit separation of linkage chemistry by DFT. The good performance of the descriptor-based RF is consistent with Orhan et al., whose RF with energy- and charge-based descriptors predicted low-pressure CO_{{2}} uptake in MOFs with R^{{2}} > 0.9{cite("orhan2023")}. It contrasts with the strong GNN results of Choudhary et al. and Kang et al.{cite("choudhary2022", "kang2023")}, but those models were trained on 10^{{4}}–10^{{5}} hypothetical MOFs, whereas only {n} experimental COFs are available here; the MOFTransformer also includes an energy grid of the pore, which supports our interpretation that pore information, not the atomic graph alone, carries most of the signal. Finally, the low median selectivity of unfunctionalised COFs is consistent with the experimental shift towards amine-functionalised COFs for DAC{cite("lyu2022", "zhou2024")}. The contrast between the weak effect of linkage chemistry in our simulations and the large effect of grafted amines in experiment is not a contradiction: chemisorption is outside the scope of a physisorption force field, and pore-wall functionalisation differs from linkage chemistry.')
H2('Practical implications')
P(f'For materials design, the results suggest prioritising narrow-pore COFs and then functionalising their pore walls, rather than searching among linkage chemistries alone; the ranked list in Supplementary Data 1 provides candidate scaffolds. For computational screening, a descriptor-based model trained on about 10% of the frameworks can triage the rest and recover most top performers after simulating a small fraction of them, which is most valuable when the downstream calculation is expensive, for example mixture GCMC with DFT-derived charges, DFT-quality adsorption data{cite("sriram2024")}, or process-level evaluation of working capacity, cost and life-cycle impact{cite("huck2014", "farmahini2021", "charalambous2024")}. Under the present model, few unfunctionalised COFs reach selectivities typical of DAC sorbents, but because the strongest sites are underestimated this conclusion is provisional.')
H2('Limitations and future work')
P(f'Each limitation of this study points to a specific next step. (i) Frameworks were treated as rigid, perfect crystals in their deposited geometries, which neglects flexibility, stacking disorder and defects; simulations on DFT-optimised and disordered structures would test how much these factors change the ranking. (ii) EQeq charges and generic LJ parameters underestimate binding at N-rich sites, as the experimental comparison shows; the top candidates should be re-evaluated with DFT-derived charges and system-specific or ML-parametrised force fields{cite("devos2023", "korolev2021")}. (iii) The Henry selectivity ignores competition at finite loading and the effect of water, which is decisive for flue gas and important for DAC{cite("boyd2019")}; mixture GCMC with humidity and process modelling{cite("charalambous2024")} are needed before any candidate is recommended for synthesis. (iv) The DFT cluster models capture single-site binding of five motifs, without confinement and with one site per motif; periodic DFT on whole frameworks would remove this restriction. (v) SchNet was trained with a limited, untuned budget imposed by CPU-only computation; tuned GNNs and hybrid models that add pore descriptors or pore nodes to the graph should be compared on the same folds. (vi) The experimental comparison is limited by the few COFs with low-pressure CO_{{2}} isotherms measured under comparable conditions; targeted measurements on the top-ranked frameworks would provide the most informative test of the predictions.')
""")
# ---- conclusions
cut("P(f'We have mapped the CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} Henry selectivity", "H1('Data availability')", r"""P(f'We have mapped the CO_{{2}}/N_{{2}} Henry selectivity and CO_{{2}} adsorption enthalpy of {n} experimentally reported COFs with an open workflow that combines Widom insertion, dispersion-corrected DFT on linkage motifs and machine learning. The scientific contribution is threefold. First, within a rigid-framework, generic force-field model, pore confinement rather than linkage chemistry sets the scale of selectivity, and DFT shows that the linkage motifs differ in intrinsic CO_{{2}} affinity by only a few kJ mol^{{−1}}. Second, for a data set of this size, a random forest on physical descriptors predicts selectivity more accurately than a SchNet graph network, and the ablation shows that the advantage comes from explicit pore information. Third, the leading candidates remain at the top when sampling is extended and when the force field and charges are varied, although their absolute selectivities are uncertain. For COF development, the practical implication is to start from narrow-pore scaffolds and functionalise their pore walls; for ML-based screening, it is to encode pore geometry explicitly and to use models as triage tools that reduce, rather than replace, simulation. Future work should re-evaluate the top candidates with DFT-derived charges and improved force fields, include water and finite-loading competition, test tuned and hybrid graph models, and compare predictions with targeted low-pressure measurements.')
""")
